Find compiler plugins relative to the compiler, not to the driver.

swift-driver assumes it is installed in the same `bin` directory as
`swift-frontend`, and derives the toolchain root — where compiler plugins and
the in-process plugin server live — from its own executable path. Nixpkgs
builds swift-driver as its own derivation, so that root points into the
swift-driver store path, which contains no plugins at all. Every macro then
fails to expand:

  error: external macro implementation type 'SwiftMacros.TaskLocalMacro' could
  not be found for macro 'TaskLocal()'; plugin for module 'SwiftMacros' not
  found

The Swift wrapper already tells the driver where the frontend is, through
`SWIFT_DRIVER_SWIFT_FRONTEND_EXEC`; use that when it is set. This only changes
behaviour when the variable is present, so a normal toolchain layout is
unaffected.

